WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.220
Speaker 1: There is something super interesting&nbsp;
going on in South Carolina where failed former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:10.080
President Donald Trump is really struggling&nbsp;
to find state lawmakers willing to show up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.940
at this stupid rally event he has planned for&nbsp;
this weekend and support him on stage. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.140
let me kind of remind you what's going on.&nbsp;
Trump's running for president again in 2024.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:24.000
He announced relatively early. He basically hasn't&nbsp;
left his house since the announcement in November,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.520
but now he is starting events. One such event is&nbsp;
in South Carolina this weekend. Our correspondent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.180
Luke Beasley is going to be there. He will be&nbsp;
interviewing people. Should be pretty interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.740
content that we will have for you as a result.&nbsp;
Now, Trump doesn't seem to be struggling to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:43.860
get federal level lawmakers who represent South&nbsp;
Carolina to go and stand with him at the rally.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:50.460
But the state lawmakers are hilariously not&nbsp;
super thrilled about participating. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:55.860
is a Rolling Stone article so sad. Trump's South&nbsp;
Carolina rally getting little support from state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:02.160
lawmakers despite making numerous pleading&nbsp;
phone calls. The former president's team is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:08.580
hitting a wall in the state, writes Peter Wade&nbsp;
for Rolling Stone. The Washington Post reports&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:13.080
that Trump's team has been making fleeting phone&nbsp;
calls to potential supporters across the state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:18.840
but many are responding with less than enthusiasm&nbsp;
because South Carolina lawmakers are not yet ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:24.300
to commit to a candidate, especially when two of&nbsp;
the state's Republicans are eyeing presidential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:28.980
runs themselves. That's South Carolina. Former&nbsp;
South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley and current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:35.040
South Carolina Senator Tim Scott. The article&nbsp;
also mentions Lindsey Graham and Henry McMaster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:41.220
are expected to show up and show support.&nbsp;
This is really, really interesting because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:45.660
if you look at polling in South Carolina, you&nbsp;
find that the majority of South Carolina's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:52.440
residents disapprove of Trump. And to a certain&nbsp;
degree, it seems as though the state politicians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:59.640
people in the state House, state Senate are either&nbsp;
on the same page in their disapproval for Trump or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.080
are kind of reading the tea leaves and saying,&nbsp;
well, I don't know that I want to get ahead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:10.200
myself and to support Trump and be publicly seen&nbsp;
and tied to Trump when many of my constituents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:14.220
don't seem to be big fans. We don't even know&nbsp;
if Trump will be the nominee. We don't know what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:19.620
things will look like come next year. And so you&nbsp;
end up with this situation where only five of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:25.440
state's Republican senators and representatives&nbsp;
are publicly supporting Trump. Some, like Jeff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:31.320
Duncan, are pushing back against his policies&nbsp;
and have been critical of Trump's trade policy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:35.640
for example, or have said Trump hasn't been doing&nbsp;
the right things to make the immigration system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:41.040
more fair or whatever the case may be. And then&nbsp;
some state reps from South Carolina have gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:47.460
even further than simply not voicing support.&nbsp;
You've got, for example, like Nancy Mace, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:53.460
is trying to pass legislation that undermines some&nbsp;
of Trump's policy ideas, like she wants to protect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.460 --> 00:03:00.780
so-called Dreamers from deportation, whereas&nbsp;
Trump was open to deporting Dreamers. So this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:06.240
is potentially going to be a problem for Trump as&nbsp;
this campaign tries to get going. There have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:12.420
advisers to Trump just blanketing South Carolina,&nbsp;
trying to find people to come and support him. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:18.540
the important thing to remember and we learned&nbsp;
this from the Democratic primary. Just because a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:26.520
candidate can have a rally with a few thousand&nbsp;
people. It doesn't necessarily mean that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:32.160
are well-liked statewide. And, you know, one of&nbsp;
the things we experienced during the Democratic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:37.740
primary was that if you base your estimates of&nbsp;
support for Bernie Sanders based on Twitter and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:45.480
Reddit and I was one of his supporters, you had&nbsp;
a much inflated sense of Bernie support within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:51.180
the Democratic Party. And then when voting&nbsp;
actually started taking place, you got a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:58.080
very different sense. And we know ultimately how&nbsp;
that went down. That all being said, the polls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.940
as we reviewed yesterday, are still looking pretty&nbsp;
good for Trump in the Republican primary. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:08.580
was a period there in early December where&nbsp;
Ron DeSantis was really doing quite well in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:14.400
the polling. But it does appear, at least right&nbsp;
now, that Donald Trump has a commanding lead in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.200
just about every Republican primary poll of&nbsp;
substance. That being said, nobody else has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.460
announced Trump is being put up in polls against&nbsp;
people who aren't even officially running yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:27.480
And that could mean something quite different&nbsp;
once we get more announcements. So we're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:31.560
to follow it. I will not be covering the rally&nbsp;
this weekend. I'm actually going to be away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.260
but Luke Beazley's going to be there and&nbsp;
then we'll have clips for when I am back

